# LIVE REGRESSION FIXTURE (graduated 2026-08-29) — CoW 2G, a user
# `&self`-mutator receiver in a loop.
#
# WHAT IT WAS. The in-body materialize for a user `&self`-mutating method fired
# via the TYPED fn_sigs first-param-MutPtr lookup, but the shared prescan was an
# UNTYPED AST walk: it could not resolve that a user method NAME (`compact`)
# mutates its receiver, so the pre-header materialize did not fire and the
# private copy was thrown away every iteration. The `MUTATING_METHODS` fallback
# caught user methods NAMED like a builtin mutator (push/pop/append/…) while
# arbitrarily-named ones (compact/bump) stayed uncovered — which is the same
# defect that made a rename decide memory safety in
# `user_mutator_method_name_decides_memory_safety.gg`.
#
# WHAT IT IS NOW. The prescan reads the semantic pass's typed per-receiver
# classification (`semantic::safety::ReceiverMutations`), so `Buf.compact(&self)`
# marks its bare-param receiver whatever it is called, and the loop-carried
# pre-header materialize fires ONCE.
#
# Asserts the CORRECT loop-carried output: private copy [1,2,3,4] compacted
# twice → 3, 2, final 2, caller 4. ggdef adjudicates the same 3,2,2,4, and so
# does the self-host lowerer lane (which was already correct here — a "reference
# lags the self-host" data point, now closed).
#
# The RECEIVER IS A BARE LOCAL PARAM, which is what makes this cell load-bearing:
# a self-rooted-only classifier never marks it, so this fixture separates the
# shipped per-receiver answer from that narrower shape.
struct Buf:
    Vector[int] data

equip Buf:
    void compact(&self):
        self.data.pop()

void f(Buf b):
    int i = 0
    while i < 2:
        b.compact()
        print(b.data.len())
        i = i + 1
    print(b.data.len())

void main():
    Buf x = Buf([1, 2, 3, 4])
    f(x)
    print(x.data.len())
